---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import AnimationPlayer from "../components/AnimationPlayer";
import Hero from "../components/Hero";
import CoverageMap from "../components/CoverageMap.astro";
import { REPO_URL, TEAM, repoFile, withBase } from "../data/site";
import LIMITS from "../../../shared/limits.json";

const title = "Wendy's Water Wonder";
const description =
  "A low-cost water-quality station and early-warning system for water points in Turkana, Kenya. Built at the BCG Platinion hackathon Fighting World Hunger, October 2026.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <header class="site-header">
    <div class="wrap">
      <a class="brand" href="#top"><img src={withBase("favicon.svg")} width="28" height="28" alt="" /> Wendy's Water Wonder</a>
      <nav class="site-nav" aria-label="Sections">
        <a href="#station">Station</a>
        <a href="#network">Network</a>
        <a href="#system">System</a>
        <a href="#demo">Dashboard</a>
        <a href="#build">Build it</a>
        <a href="#team">Team</a>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main id="top">
    <Hero
      client:load
      title={title}
      lead="A low-cost station at the well measures the water, warns people on site and tells the right people what to do, even where the mobile network is patchy."
      image={withBase("og.jpg")}
      imageAlt="The station: a Raspberry Pi with a breadboard, three water sensors and status lights; a water sample has turned the red light on."
      repoUrl={REPO_URL}
    />
    <p class="wrap notice">Built in two days. This is not a certified water-testing device, and all limits are placeholders until they are agreed with water experts.</p>

    <section id="problem">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">The problem</p>
        <h2>Bad water is noticed too late</h2>
        <p class="lead">In Turkana, many people depend on boreholes, shallow wells and open pans. Wendy, the field officer in our scenario, looks after many of them.</p>
        <div class="cards">
          <div class="card"><h3>It changes unnoticed</h3><p>A water point can turn salty or contaminated, and nobody knows until people are already drinking from it.</p></div>
          <div class="card"><h3>Nobody can be everywhere</h3><p>Field staff can only visit each point now and then. Lab tests take time.</p></div>
          <div class="card"><h3>The network is patchy</h3><p>Many water points are far from the next cell tower, so data has to wait until there is a connection.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="how">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">The idea</p>
        <h2>Measure, warn, act</h2>
        <ol class="steps">
          <li><strong>Measure at the well</strong><span>Salt, turbidity and pH every 30 seconds.</span></li>
          <li><strong>Warn on site</strong><span>Three lights work even without a network.</span></li>
          <li><strong>Send when possible</strong><span>A buffer keeps the readings until a connection is there.</span></li>
          <li><strong>Decide in the cloud</strong><span>Weather, lab tests and the other water points come together.</span></li>
          <li><strong>Alert and reset</strong><span>The right people get an SMS; only a person switches a warning off.</span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="station">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">The station</p>
        <h2>A Raspberry Pi, three sensors and a traffic light</h2>
        <p class="lead">Watch what happens when salt, mud or acid get into the water. The animation runs live in your browser; the chapters jump straight to each situation.</p>
        <AnimationPlayer client:visible film="station" />
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Light</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Switched on by</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><span class="dot" style="background:#f8fafc"></span>white</td><td>The station runs and measures nothing unusual. <b>Not</b> "safe".</td><td>the station software</td></tr>
              <tr><td><span class="dot" style="background:#facc15"></span>yellow</td><td>Suspicious: probably not drinkable as it is</td><td>salt over {LIMITS.tds_ppm.yellow} ppm, pH outside {LIMITS.ph.min}–{LIMITS.ph.max}, turbidity over {LIMITS.turbidity_ntu.yellow} NTU, or a germ risk from the cloud</td></tr>
              <tr><td><span class="dot" style="background:#ef4444"></span>red</td><td>Not drinkable, whatever you do: boiling makes salty water worse</td><td>salt over {LIMITS.tds_ppm.red} ppm, or the cloud says salt is red</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="fine">Yellow and red only go up on their own. They go off when a person who checked the water point presses "Reset water" in the dashboard.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="network">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">The place</p>
        <h2>Turkana: water far from the network</h2>
        <p class="lead">We put open data on water points and cell towers on one map. It shows why the station has to work offline first.</p>
        <CoverageMap>
          <a class="btn" href={withBase("map/turkana-map.html")}>Open the full interactive map (German)</a>
        </CoverageMap>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="system">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">The system</p>
        <h2>From the well to the phone</h2>
        <p class="lead">The station sends its buffer to Azure whenever it has a connection. The cloud combines the readings with weather and lab tests, alerts people and sends its verdict back to the station's lights.</p>
        <AnimationPlayer client:visible film="architecture" />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="demo">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">Try it</p>
        <h2>Wendy's dashboard</h2>
        <p class="lead">The real dashboard Wendy works with, running here on data recorded with the cloud's risk logic. Pick a situation at the bottom left, click a water point, set a colour or reset it.</p>
        <div class="dash-embed">
          <iframe src={withBase("dashboard/index.html")} title="Wendy's dashboard, demo with recorded data" loading="lazy"></iframe>
        </div>
        <div class="buttons" style="margin-top:16px">
          <a class="btn" href={withBase("dashboard/index.html")}>Open the dashboard full screen</a>
        </div>
        <p class="fine">This is the original dashboard from <code>cloud/static/map.html</code>. For the demo, Azure Maps is replaced by MapLibre with Esri satellite imagery, and the cloud API by data from <code>cloud/export_demo_state.py</code>. Nothing is sent. Positions near Lodwar and all limits are placeholders; only well07 was a real station.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="build">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">Build it</p>
        <h2>Everything is open</h2>
        <div class="cards">
          <div class="card">
            <h3>The station</h3>
            <p>Raspberry Pi 5, MCP3008 ADC, CQRobot TDS meter, DFRobot-style turbidity sensor, DFRobot Gravity pH meter, three LEDs and a few resistors.</p>
            <p style="margin-top:12px"><a href={repoFile("docs/hardware.md")}>Parts, wiring and calibration</a></p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>The cloud</h3>
            <p>Azure IoT Hub, Functions, Table Storage and Maps, plus Open-Meteo for the weather. SMS via Azure Communication Services or Twilio.</p>
            <p style="margin-top:12px"><a href={repoFile("cloud/README.md")}>Set up and deploy</a></p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Without hardware</h3>
            <p>The tests, the risk logic and the uploader in fake mode run on any laptop.</p>
            <p style="margin-top:12px"><code>pytest</code> · <code>python3 pi/uploader.py --fake --dry-run</code></p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <h3 style="margin-top:48px">Ideas for next steps</h3>
        <ul class="muted">
          <li>Cheaper hardware: a Raspberry Pi Pico or ESP32 instead of the Pi, with solar power</li>
          <li>An SMS uplink over 2G or LoRa where there is no mobile data</li>
          <li>Africa's Talking instead of Twilio for SMS in Kenya</li>
          <li>Predict pump failures from usage and sensor data</li>
          <li>Validate the limits and the germ-risk model with water experts and real lab tests</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="honest">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">Honestly</p>
        <h2>What is real and what is a placeholder</h2>
        <div class="cards">
          <div class="card"><h3>Real</h3><p>The station with three sensors, offline buffer, lights and upload to Azure; the risk logic, outlook, dashboard and SMS alerts; the weather data; the human reset; the Turkana map from open data.</p></div>
          <div class="card"><h3>Placeholder</h3><p>The salt limits ({LIMITS.tds_ppm.yellow} / {LIMITS.tds_ppm.red} / {LIMITS.tds_ppm.livestock_red} mg/L) and the germ-risk weights; the positions of the 14 water points near Lodwar; 13 of the 14 points are simulated; turbidity in NTU is only a rough estimate.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="team">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">Team</p>
        <h2>Who built it</h2>
        <ul class="team">
          {TEAM.map((m) => (
            <li>{m.github ? <a href={`https://github.com/${m.github}`}>{m.name}</a> : m.name}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <p class="muted" style="margin-top:24px">Thanks to BCG Platinion for the hackathon and to Welthungerhilfe for the case.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer class="site-footer">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p>Code: <a href={repoFile("LICENSE")}>MIT</a> · Documentation and animations: <a href={repoFile("LICENSE-docs")}>CC BY 4.0</a> · Map data: © OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL), Water Point Data Exchange (CC BY 4.0), geoBoundaries · Weather: Open-Meteo, Azure Maps</p>
      <p>The animations are made with <a href="https://www.remotion.dev">Remotion</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </footer>
</Base>
